{:steps
 [{:name "select-and-create",
   :type :session,
   :tools ["read" "bash" "edit" "write"],
   :skills ["incidental-complexity-finder" "gordian" "code-shaper"],
   :thinking-level :high,
   :contributions
   [{:type :template,
     :text
     "You are executing the incidental-complexity simplification workflow in this repository. This workflow runs entirely in the invoking session's current inherited worktree. It selects the single highest incidental-complexity unit, captures baselines, creates a constrained behaviour-preserving refactor task in the current worktree, and emits a structured handoff. Later workflow steps review the design, create/review the plan, establish a pre-simplification characterization-test net, run a baseline/diff gate, and only then delegate simplification implementation and review in the same inherited worktree.\n\nRequired procedure:\n\n1. Confirm the current worktree context.\n   - Run `git status --short --branch` and treat the current branch/worktree as the intended refactor location.\n   - Do NOT call `work-on` and do NOT create or switch to another worktree. The caller is responsible for invoking this workflow from the intended isolated branch/worktree.\n   - If the current checkout has an ambiguous or unsafe state for an autonomous refactor, stop and report that clearly instead of switching worktrees.\n\n2. Select the target with the `incidental-complexity-finder` skill.\n   - Apply the `incidental-complexity-finder` skill to choose the single highest incidental-complexity executable unit (the `gap = lcc-total / max(cc, 1)` method, the `lcc-total >= 5.0 and gap >= 2.0` qualification filter, and the top-5 essential-vs-incidental judgment guard).\n   - Capture the chosen target's evidence (ns, var, arity, file, line range, lcc-total with per-dimension burdens, cc, gap, the local findings, and the coverage hint).\n\n3. Early stop when no unit qualifies.\n   - If no unit qualifies (nothing meets `lcc-total >= 5.0 and gap >= 2.0`, or none of the top 5 pass the judgment guard), STOP.\n   - Do NOT create a task.\n   - Do NOT create or switch worktrees.\n   - Final response must clearly say that no unit qualified, listing the candidates considered.\n   - Do not emit a `munera_task_path:` line in the no-target response.\n   - End the final response with exactly `PASS_STATUS: ACTIONABLE_FEEDBACK` so the workflow deterministically skips every downstream design/plan/test-net/implementation step and completes directly.\n\n4. Allocate the task id and create the task directory in the current worktree.\n   - Allocate the next canonical Munera task id by scanning the CURRENT WORKTREE's `munera/open/` and `munera/closed/`: `NNN = max(NNN over open/ union closed/) + 1`, zero-padded to >= 3 digits.\n   - Create `munera/open/NNN-slug/design.md` for the generated refactor task (slug derived from the target), using the contract in step 6.\n\n5. Capture two `before` baselines into the task directory (run from the current worktree root):\n   - `before-local.json` — `bb gordian local --json` (bare, NO `--sort`) — the per-unit comprehension-burden baseline. Write it to `munera/open/NNN-slug/before-local.json`.\n   - `before-diagnose.edn` — `bb gordian diagnose --edn` — the architectural gate baseline for `gordian gate --baseline`. Write it to `munera/open/NNN-slug/before-diagnose.edn`.\n   - Note: `before-local.json` uses a DIFFERENT invocation from the selector's `local --sort total --json` ranking call; because the Phase-1 before/after comparison is keyed by `(ns, var, arity, line)`, sort order is irrelevant to baseline validity.\n   - Path resolution: Phase 1 runs `gordian gate`/`gordian local` from the worktree root (cwd), where a bare filename does not resolve. The generated `design.md` must therefore reference each baseline by its worktree-root-relative task-dir path — `munera/open/NNN-slug/before-diagnose.edn` and `munera/open/NNN-slug/before-local.json`. Record the concrete task path in the generated `design.md` so Phase-1 commands use the resolvable path rather than a bare filename.\n\n6. Generated `design.md` — the two-phase behaviour-preserving contract.\n   The generated task is a behaviour-preserving refactor (the `refactor_minimal_semantics_spec_tests` formalism), executed in two ordered phases. Its `design.md` must state:\n   - The target unit and the captured incidental-complexity evidence.\n   - Blast radius: the target unit PLUS the minimal surrounding helpers required to decomplect it; no unrelated cleanup. The net-burden acceptance below keeps this honest.\n   - Constraint: behaviour is identical — meta/spec are unchanged; existing test expectations are not weakened.\n\n   Phase 0 — establish a test safety net (gate before any refactor).\n   A behaviour-preserving refactor is unverifiable without tests that would catch a behaviour change, so refactoring is gated on sufficient coverage:\n   - Assess existing coverage of the target's observable behaviour (tests that exercise the unit directly or transitively), judged against `{nominal, edge, boundary}` per the project Test formalism.\n   - If insufficient, add characterization tests capturing CURRENT observable behaviour (state/outputs, never interactions; per `testing-without-mocks`). Adding tests that document existing behaviour does not change behaviour or spec — it strengthens the proof.\n   - These tests must be GREEN against the unmodified code before any refactoring begins.\n   - If the unit cannot be characterized safely (e.g. an untestable side-effect tangle), the task records that finding and either (a) first introduces a minimal seam to make it testable, or (b) is closed with the finding (scope drift -> close per Munera). No refactor proceeds without a green net.\n\n   Phase 1 — refactor under the green net. Decomplect the target with minimal, local, root-cause changes (not superficial extraction).\n   Objective acceptance criteria:\n   - Burden reduction (A5 — named comparison source). Re-run `bb gordian local --json` from the worktree root and compare against the stored `munera/open/NNN-slug/before-local.json` captured in this step — that file is the single authoritative baseline for every \"decreased\" check (not the selector's emitted evidence, not a fresh pre-refactor recompute). The target unit's `lcc-total` (keyed by `(ns, var, arity, line)` — the same unique key the selector's join uses; `line` disambiguates same-named null-arity `defmethod` units that share `(ns, var, arity)`, e.g. the 51 `execute-effect!` defmethods) decreased versus its `before-local.json` value.\n   - Net burden (A2 — relocation guard, per-unit). A2 rejects ONLY a superficial/inverted extraction — \"I reduced the target's burden by relocating its tangle into a new seam or an existing sibling.\" It does NOT re-police the target's own reduction (that is A5) and must NOT sum normalized per-unit burdens: Gordian's per-unit transform is concave / sub-additive, so summing across the target plus its extracted seam double-counts decomplection's benefit as a cost, making a net-sum gate provably unsatisfiable by any genuine extraction. A2 is two pure per-unit inequalities against the ORIGINAL target's burden `B`, with no margin (no slack threshold, no jitter buffer). Let `B := before(target)` — the original target unit's `lcc-total` read from the committed `munera/open/NNN-slug/before-local.json` baseline (an immutable published anchor; NOT a recomputed `after(target)`).\n     - A2a (new pieces are genuine, not a relocated tangle): every physical after-row `u` whose key is NEW (`before-max(k) = 0`) satisfies `after(u) < B`. Each extracted seam is strictly simpler than the original whole it was carved from.\n     - A2b (no collateral ceiling breach in existing rows): every physical after-row `u` whose key is pre-existing and below the ceiling (`0 < before-max(k) < B`) satisfies `after(u) < B`. A key already at or above the ceiling (`before-max(k) >= B`) through no fault of this change is EXEMPT — relocating into an already-oversized sibling cannot make it cross a threshold it already exceeds, and any genuine architectural worsening there is caught by A3.\n     How A2 is mechanically checked (a deterministic numeric procedure over two JSON artifacts — the same KIND of objective check as A3, not agent judgement; run from the worktree root): (1) `B := before(target)` — the target's `lcc-total` in `munera/open/NNN-slug/before-local.json`, located by its line-bearing `(ns, var, arity, line)` identity (the same row A5 governs). (2) Recompute after burdens with `bb gordian local --json` (bare, no `--sort`). (3) Group BOTH JSONs by the line-insensitive key `k = (ns, var, arity)`. A2's atomic unit is the PHYSICAL defunit row `u` (one source occurrence, carrying its own `after(u)`); the key `k` only classifies a row's before-side status, never merges after-rows. For each key `k`, `before-max(k)` := the maximum `lcc-total` among before-rows carrying `k` (`0` if `k` has no before-row). `before-max` — not a sum, not a per-line pairing — is the only before-side quantity A2a/A2b consume, keeping the check well-defined even when `k` is non-unique (e.g. the 51 `execute-effect!` defmethods sharing one null-arity key, disambiguated only by `line`). A2 keys line-insensitively on purpose: a refactor moves line numbers for almost every unit, so a line-sensitive join would collapse the touched set into \"everything changed\". (4) Form the touched set `T`: a physical after-row `u` with key `k` is in `T` iff `k` is NEW (no before-row) OR the multiset of `lcc-total` values over `k`'s before-rows differs from the multiset over `k`'s after-rows (an order-insensitive set comparison — never a sum). `T`-membership is a reporting/efficiency filter, not load-bearing for soundness: an untouched row has `after(u) = before(u) <= before-max(k)`, so it auto-satisfies A2a/A2b when `before-max(k) < B` and is exempt when `before-max(k) >= B`. Then remove ONLY the target's own physical row (identified by its line-bearing `(ns, var, arity, line)` key — exactly one row, never the whole `(ns, var, arity)` group); its reduction is A5's job. When the target shares its line-insensitive key with siblings (the defmethod case), those siblings STAY in `T` and remain policed by A2a/A2b, so relocating the tangle into a key-sharing sibling still trips the ceiling. (5) A2a: for every `u` in `T` whose key has no before-row (`before-max(k) = 0`), assert `after(u) < B`. (6) A2b: for every `u` in `T` whose key is pre-existing with `0 < before-max(k) < B`, assert `after(u) < B` (keys with `before-max(k) >= B` are exempt). Every clause is a numeric `<` over fields read directly from the two JSON files — no threshold to tune, no interpretation step. There is no single `bb gordian` subcommand for this join-and-compare today; perform the spelled-out procedure (a dedicated command is a reasonable but separate follow-up). Why this is sound: splitting a tangle of burden `B` into pieces each `< B` is exactly what decomplection does, so A2a is always satisfiable by a genuine split; fragmenting into many tiny units raises the target's own dependency/working-set burden and fails A5; helper-chasing is flagged by A3; scope sprawl fails the blast-radius/minimality criteria. A2 need not (and must not) re-police total burden.\n   - Architectural no-regression (A3 — enforcing gate flags). Run `bb gordian gate --baseline munera/open/NNN-slug/before-diagnose.edn --fail-on new-cycles,new-high-findings --max-new-medium-findings 0`. The bare `gate --baseline` only EVALUATES checks; the `--fail-on` flag is what makes new cycles and new high findings FAIL (non-zero exit), and `--max-new-medium-findings 0` enforces the \"no new medium findings\" half of the claim. The gate must PASS (exit 0) with these flags.\n   - The Phase 0 characterization tests and all existing tests for the affected area are GREEN (same expectations as before the refactor).\n   - The change is minimal, local, and decomplecting.\n\n   Autonomy note: this generated task's design is intentionally authored autonomously (objective, narrow: fixed target + preserve behaviour + objective acceptance); the review-task-design loop iterates ambiguity/architecture/inconsistency in place of live user collaboration.\n\n7. Commit the task creation on the current branch.\n   - Commit the new `munera/open/NNN-slug/` task directory (including both baseline files) on the current branch, so delegated lifecycle child sessions inherit the same worktree and can resolve the emitted task path.\n\n8. Emit a structured handoff as your final response.\n   - Output a compact Markdown summary with these headings exactly:\n     - `## Selection Outcome`\n     - `## Munera Task`\n     - `## Handoff Data`\n   - Under `## Selection Outcome`, summarize the chosen target and its evidence (ns/var/arity, file, line range, lcc-total, cc, gap), and the coverage hint.\n   - Under `## Munera Task`, give the task id/slug and the baselines captured.\n   - Under `## Handoff Data`, include this machine-friendly bullet line:\n     - `munera_task_path:` — the `munera/open/NNN-slug` task path.\n   - You may include `worktree_path:` as informational context, but downstream lifecycle execution must rely on inherited session worktree rather than calling `work-on`.\n   - If a field is not available, omit it rather than inventing it.\n   - End the final response with exactly `PASS_STATUS: REVIEW_COMPLETE` so the workflow deterministically continues to design review.\n\nExecution constraints:\n- Work only on the single chosen unit from the `incidental-complexity-finder` selection.\n- Prefer root-cause simplification framing over superficial extraction in the generated design.\n- Do NOT call `work-on`.\n- Do NOT create or switch worktrees.\n- Do NOT create a task when no unit qualifies.\n- Do NOT push or open a PR; this workflow ends with a completed, reviewed task on the current local branch (the user decides on PR).\n\nInput:\n{{input}}\n\n   Mandatory task-local proof artifacts and selector uncertainty recording.\n   - `select-and-create` is the first writer for the task-local `coverage-map.md`. Create and commit an initial `munera/open/NNN-slug/coverage-map.md` scaffold before emitting the target-present handoff. Pending or unknown coverage/test counts must be represented explicitly as pending; do not omit required fields.\n   - The generated `design.md` and `coverage-map.md` must name root-relative proof artifacts: `before-local.json`, `before-diagnose.edn`, `after-local.json`, `incidental-burden-check.edn`, `incidental-gate.edn`, `coverage-map.md`, and `characterization-baseline.edn`.\n   - Before making baseline or selector proof claims, parse-check `before-local.json` as JSON and require a top-level `units` array; parse-check `before-diagnose.edn` as EDN before baseline/gate claims. Exit-0 unreadable JSON/EDN is a failure, not proof.\n   - `coverage-map.md` minimum fields: target identity (namespace, var, arity, line range, source path, selected `(ns, var, arity, line)` key); selector proof (`lcc-total`, `cc`, `gap`); top-5 guard decision for the chosen target; rejected essential false positives before the chosen target when relevant; authoritative test commands and focused test namespaces/files; existing coverage and newly added characterization tests grouped by nominal/edge/boundary observable behaviour; coverage-gap dispositions (`FIXABLE_GAPS`, `INFEASIBLE`, or reviewed-complete notes); latest recorded test/assertion counts with command/output reference; relationship to `characterization-baseline.edn`; and whether any coverage-phase production seam was introduced.\n   - The generated task must record the top-5 guard evidence table when qualifying candidates exist, with fields ns, var, arity, line, `lcc-total`, `cc`, `gap`, guard decision (`accepted-incidental`, `rejected-essential`, or `rejected-uncertain`), and one-sentence rationale. If no higher candidate was rejected before the chosen target, say so explicitly.\n   - Mark an accepted target as marginal when `lcc-total < 5.5`, `gap < 2.2`, a higher-gap candidate was rejected as essential, or the guard rationale depends on weak evidence, ambiguous findings, or uncertain coverage hints. For marginal targets, state the concern, falsification evidence, and scope/coverage review questions before planning.\n   - Record `coverage-map.md` lifecycle ownership in the generated task: `coverage-review` updates coverage/test-net fields; `coverage-fix` updates it for added tests or seams; `diff-gate` records coverage-phase classification and relationship to `characterization-baseline.edn`; `incidental-validation-capture` records final Gordian proof references; `proof-sync` performs final stale-proof synchronization; and `final-summary` reads it as committed proof authority.",
     :vars {"input" {:from :workflow-input}}}],
   :judge
   {:type :invoke,
    :operation "workflow/pass-status-routing",
    :args
    {:text
     {:from {:step "select-and-create", :output :final-llm-reply}},
     :allowed-statuses ["ACTIONABLE_FEEDBACK" "REVIEW_COMPLETE"]}},
   :on {"DONE" {:goto "extract-task-path"}, "REPEAT" {:goto :done}}}
  {:name "extract-task-path",
   :type :session,
   :tools ["read" "bash" "edit" "write"],
   :contributions
   [{:type :source, :from {:step "select-and-create", :yield :text}}
    {:type :template,
     :text
     "Validate the target-present handoff from `select-and-create` and extract the generated Munera task path.\n\nRules:\n1. Read only the `select-and-create` handoff provided in context for the task path.\n2. It must contain exactly one line matching `munera_task_path: munera/open/NNN-slug`, where NNN is zero-padded digits and slug is kebab-case path text with no spaces.\n3. On success, respond with ONLY that root-relative path and no other prose. Example: `munera/open/220-example-task`.\n4. On malformed input, missing path, multiple paths, absolute path, non-open path, or non-root-relative value, do not guess. Respond with a terse extraction failure summary. Do not invent or read task-local artifacts. The judge will route this to `terminal-stop-malformed-task-path`.\n\nInput handoff:\n{{handoff}}",
     :vars
     {"handoff" {:from {:step "select-and-create", :yield :text}}}}],
   :judge
   {:type :invoke,
    :operation "workflow/munera-open-task-path-routing",
    :args
    {:text
     {:from {:step "extract-task-path", :output :final-llm-reply}}}},
   :on
   {"DONE" {:goto "review-task-design"},
    "REPEAT" {:goto "terminal-stop-malformed-task-path"}}}
  {:name "terminal-stop-malformed-task-path",
   :type :session,
   :tools ["read" "bash"],
